id: pm-m4ikkz
title: "MCP tool surface is a static snapshot: blind to runtime workspace schema and extension commands, and custom fields are unreachable through it"
description: "The CLI derives its surface from the active workspace at runtime: pm schema add-field mints a real CLI flag, pm schema add-type/add-status widen the accepted domains, installed extensions register real commands, and pm contracts reports all of it. The MCP server does none of that. tools/list is a static 31-tool snapshot that never consults the workspace, so the agent surface that most agents actually drive is strictly less capable than the CLI, and is blind to exactly the customization the product's thesis rests on. Measured against dist build 2026.7.25 on a sandbox workspace carrying a custom type Experiment, a custom status needs_review, a custom field hypothesis, and an installed extension registering 'myext ping'.\n\nFindings, in increasing severity:\n\n1. Custom item types and custom statuses are usable but undeclared. tools/list contains no occurrence of 'Experiment' or 'needs_review'; a pm_create call passing them succeeds and persists correctly. So they work only if the agent already knows they exist from outside the protocol.\n\n2. Extension commands are executable but undiscoverable. 'myext ping' appears in no tool. It IS reachable as pm_run with action 'myext ping' and returns the extension's payload correctly, but pm_run.action is declared as a plain string whose description embeds a hardcoded list of operation names that does not include it. There is no enum, so there is no client-side validation and no discovery path.\n\n3. Custom fields are not reachable at all. pm_create with a top-level hypothesis argument returns the warning 'Unexpected top-level argument \"hypothesis\" for pm_create. It was passed through unchanged; declared arguments are: cwd, path, author, fullChangedFields, allowMissingParent, allowDuplicate, options.' The item is created and the field is silently dropped (pm get shows hypothesis null). Passing it nested as options.hypothesis is likewise dropped. The same warning text is emitted for title, type and status in the same call, and those three DO persist, so the warning does not distinguish honored arguments from discarded ones and an agent cannot tell which happened. A field that the CLI exposes as a first-class flag is therefore write-impossible over MCP.\n\nCost context: the static tools/list already costs 40,010 bytes, roughly 10k tokens, for 31 tools, so growing it naively is not the answer; the surface should be derived and scoped to what the workspace actually has.\n\nDistinct from pm-xwah, which derives MCP inputSchemas from the static *_FLAG_CONTRACTS tables. This item is about deriving from the RUNTIME workspace (schema registry + extension registry), which those tables cannot know. Distinct from pm-upi0, which is about unknown keys in nested options being dropped generally; finding 3 here is a declared-capability gap, not an unknown-key warning gap."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 1
tags[4]: agent-ux,"area:mcp","area:schema",extensibility
created_at: "2026-07-25T21:13:30.593Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-26T05:59:27.865Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 480
acceptance_criteria: "MCP tool schemas are derived from the active workspace runtime rather than a static snapshot: registered custom item types and statuses appear in the relevant enums, and registered custom fields are declared and settable through pm_create and pm_update with the value persisting. Extension-contributed commands are discoverable over MCP, either as tools or via a runtime-populated pm_run action domain, rather than only guessable. The unexpected-argument warning distinguishes arguments that were honored from arguments that were discarded, and discarding a value never reports it as passed through unchanged. tools/list cost is measured before and after and does not regress for a workspace with no customization. A regression test drives the MCP server against a workspace with a custom type, status, field and extension command and asserts each is both discoverable and usable."
parent: pm-mpbb
risk: medium
confidence: high
